  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nWe plot the given points in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/765083f1f2f4c2ce2cbfa6b3303eae85553a9995b937b1600bf801ab296a35d8.png)\n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $4$ units, so the distance equals $4.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/e92dca7c2cad5afed16a862369b470204634c2a5f83381ddb16fa45d8d990d7f.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $y$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/56b71bfae95faaee84362f7273eac40dd7a326f5cdd70ecb885cfcafdf1884ca.png)\n\nThe point $(-2,5)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $y$-axis, and the point $(-6,5)$ is at a distance of $6$ from the $y$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $6-2=4.$",
